---
import Layout from '@layouts/Layout.astro';
import AdSlot from '@components/AdSlot.astro';
---

<Layout
  lang="en"
  title="Palworld Boss Guide - All 9 Tower Bosses (1.0)"
  description="Palworld 1.0 boss guide. All 9 tower bosses (Zoe and Grizzbolt, Lily and Lyleen, Saya and Selyne, Auri and Shaolong, Zenara and Astralym) with Normal and Hard difficulty levels, HP, element counters and team tips."
>
  <article class="guide-content">
    <header class="page-header">
      <p class="breadcrumb"><a href="/en/guides/">Guides</a> › Boss Guide</p>
      <h1>⚔️ Palworld Boss Guide: All 9 Tower Bosses + Field Bosses</h1>
      <p class="text-secondary">Palworld 1.0 · Level cap 80 · Every tower has a Normal and a Hard difficulty</p>
    </header>

    <AdSlot position="inline" />

    <section>
      <h2>First, Understand the Two Tower Difficulties</h2>
      <p>Palworld 1.0 has <strong>9 tower bosses</strong>. Each one comes in two versions:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Normal</strong>: levelled to match your current progress, used to advance the story. The first five towers are Lv 10 / 20 / 30 / 40 / 50 on Normal.</li>
        <li><strong>Hard</strong>: a rematch at <strong>Lv 72-80</strong> with 5-10x the HP of the Normal version. This is where the 1.0 level cap of 80 actually matters.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        The final tower (the World Tree) only has a <strong>Lv 80</strong> version with no Hard
        variant - it is the intended final boss of the game. The full table below is built from
        the official paldb.cc boss data.
      </p>
    </section>

    <AdSlot position="inline" />

    <section>
      <h2>All 9 Tower Bosses</h2>
      <ol>
        <li>
          <strong>Rayne Syndicate Tower — Zoe &amp; Grizzbolt (Electric)</strong><br />
          Normal Lv 10 / HP 12,900 · Hard Lv 72 / HP 464,000
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Free Pal Alliance Tower — Lily &amp; Lyleen (Grass)</strong><br />
          Normal Lv 20 / HP 42,500 · Hard Lv 74 / HP 494,000
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Brothers of the Eternal Pyre Tower — Axel &amp; Orserk (Dragon, Electric)</strong><br />
          Normal Lv 30 / HP 53,750 · Hard Lv 76 / HP 514,800
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>PIDF Tower — Marcus &amp; Faleris (Fire)</strong><br />
          Normal Lv 40 / HP 72,900 · Hard Lv 78 / HP 526,900
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>PAL Genetic Research Unit Tower — Victor &amp; Shadowbeak (Dark)</strong><br />
          Normal Lv 50 / HP 105,000 · Hard Lv 80 / HP 570,000
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Moonflower Tower — Saya &amp; Selyne (Dark, Neutral)</strong><br />
          Normal Lv 55 / HP 234,900 · Hard Lv 80 / HP 567,300
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Feybreak Tower — Bjorn &amp; Bastigor (Ice)</strong><br />
          Normal Lv 60 / HP 310,000 · Hard Lv 80 / HP 487,500
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Azure Covenant Tower — Auri &amp; Shaolong (Dragon, Water)</strong><br />
          Normal Lv 68 / HP 327,600 · Hard Lv 80 / HP 501,600
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Final tower (location not yet named) — Zenara &amp; Astralym</strong><br />
          Lv 80 / HP 420,000 · no Hard version
        </li>
      </ol>
      <p>
        Element matchups are the general answer: bring <strong>Dragon or Ground</strong> into an
        Electric tower, <strong>Fire or Ice</strong> into a Grass tower, <strong>Electric or Ice</strong>
        into a Dragon tower, <strong>Dragon</strong> into a Dark tower, and <strong>Fire or Electric</strong>
        into an Ice tower. Hard versions run into the hundreds of thousands of HP, so pair a
        <a href="/en/tech-tree/Battle_RangeWeapon_RocketLauncher/">Rocket Launcher</a> with a
        healing Pal instead of a pure damage team.
      </p>
    </section>

    <AdSlot position="inline" />

    <section>
      <h2>Per-Boss Breakdown</h2>

      <h3>Tower 1 · Zoe &amp; Grizzbolt (Rayne Syndicate Tower)</h3>
      <p>
        The friendliest tower boss in the game - Normal is only Lv 10, so a starter team can
        clear it. Electric typing means Dragon or Ground Pals delete it instantly.
        The Hard version at Lv 72 is usually the first time players meet a real tower boss,
        so use it to learn the attack patterns before chasing the endgame towers.
      </p>

      <h3>Tower 2 · Lily &amp; Lyleen (Free Pal Alliance Tower)</h3>
      <p>
        Grass typing with two separate HP bars - the boss and its summon. Grass is weak to
        both Fire and Ice, and a fast Fire attacker such as
        <a href="/en/pals/faleris/">Faleris</a> keeps its distance while the Grass damage
        type chips away. The Hard version at Lv 74 sits just under 500k HP, so bring healing.
      </p>

      <h3>Tower 3 · Axel &amp; Orserk (Brothers of the Eternal Pyre Tower)</h3>
      <p>
        Dragon plus Electric - the first real wall for a mid-game team. Dragon resists most
        physical damage, so Electric or Ice Pals that can disable the boss first are the
        right call. Both halves are on the field at once: deal with them separately instead
        of letting the group get sandwiched.
      </p>

      <h3>Tower 4 · Marcus &amp; Faleris (PIDF Tower)</h3>
      <p>
        A Fire tower. Fire is weak to Water and Dragon, and Faleris-type Pals match up well
        here. Normal at Lv 40 is still on the standard progression pace - Pal Metal Armor
        is enough gear for this one.
      </p>

      <h3>Tower 5 · Victor &amp; Shadowbeak (PAL Genetic Research Unit Tower)</h3>
      <p>
        Dark typing, and the first tower that genuinely asks you to prepare: Normal is Lv 50.
        Dark does not fold to Electric, so lean on Dragon typing or high single-target damage
        instead. The Hard version at Lv 80 and 570,000 HP is the highest-HP tower boss in the
        game and needs max-level Pals plus the 1.0 accessories.
      </p>

      <h3>Tower 6 · Saya &amp; Selyne (Moonflower Tower)</h3>
      <p>
        Dark plus Neutral, guarding the Moonflower area. Because Neutral cannot be exploited
        with an elemental counter, this fight is about raw levels and gear rather than type
        advantage. Normal at Lv 55 with 234,900 HP is noticeably tankier than the earlier
        towers.
      </p>

      <h3>Tower 7 · Bjorn &amp; Bastigor (Feybreak Tower)</h3>
      <p>
        An Ice tower in Feybreak. Ice folds to Fire and Electric, so a
        <a href="/en/pals/grizzbolt/">Grizzbolt</a>-style Electric Pal that paralyzes first
        and then bursts the boss down is the most efficient route. Normal at Lv 60 and 310,000
        HP was the pre-1.0 graduation exam.
      </p>

      <h3>Tower 8 · Auri &amp; Shaolong (Azure Covenant Tower)</h3>
      <p>
        <strong>New in 1.0.</strong> It sits in the Azure Covenant tower in the Sunreach sky
        archipelago. Dragon plus Water, with Normal at <strong>Lv 68</strong> and 327,600 HP -
        in other words you need a Lv 68 team to fight it normally. Dragon fears Electric, and
        Water fears Electric and Ice, so an Electric Pal solves both halves of the typing at
        once. Hard pushes it straight to Lv 80 and 501,600 HP.
      </p>

      <h3>Tower 9 · Zenara &amp; Astralym (Final Tower)</h3>
      <p>
        <strong>The 1.0 final boss</strong>, found at the World Tree. It has a single Lv 80
        version with 420,000 HP. This is the level-cap fight: gear up to Ancient Series, fill
        your accessory slots with the best of the 44 accessories 1.0 added, and bring Pals that
        are level 80 and awakened through Radiant Gems. Stock up on Ancient healing items at
        your base before you go.
      </p>
    </section>

    <AdSlot position="inline" />

    <section>
      <h2>4 Special Bosses That Are Not Tower Bosses</h2>
      <p>
        Alongside the 9 towers, 1.0 added several standalone bosses that paldb also lists on
        its tower pages:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Legendary Ocean King Panthalus</strong> (Water) — Lv 70 / HP 353,220, location not yet named in the source data</li>
        <li><strong>Bewitching Lurker Dandilord</strong> (Grass, Dark) — Lv 78 / HP 6,740, found at Rotmist Root</li>
        <li><strong>Immortal Shade Silvance</strong> (Grass) — Lv 78 / HP 7,442, found at Shinespore Root</li>
        <li><strong>Highly Modified Grizzbolt</strong> (Electric) — Lv 78 / HP 6,506, found at the Forbidden Laboratory</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Their HP looks tiny next to the tower bosses, but the level is high - these are
        mechanic-driven fights rather than stat checks. Test them one at a time with a
        high-level single-target attacker like <a href="/en/pals/silvance/">Silvance</a>.
      </p>
    </section>

    <AdSlot position="inline" />

    <section>
      <h2>General Boss Prep Checklist</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Weapons: <a href="/en/tech-tree/Battle_RangeWeapon_AssaultRifle/">Assault Rifle</a> + <a href="/en/tech-tree/Battle_RangeWeapon_RocketLauncher/">Rocket Launcher</a></li>
        <li>Armour: swap to the Ancient Series at level 80</li>
        <li>Consumables: 30 <strong>Mega Spheres</strong> + 30 <strong>Giga Spheres</strong> and a full stack of healing items</li>
        <li>Team: 3 attackers + 1 healer + 1 support - not five fragile damage dealers</li>
        <li>Traps: <a href="/en/tech-tree/Special_SphereFactory_Black_Grade_02/">Megalasphereline Trap</a> to improve capture odds</li>
        <li>Levels: match the tower on Normal, and take everything to Lv 80 for Hard</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="related-links">
      <h2>Related Guides</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><a href="/en/guides/palworld-1-0-guide/">🆕 Palworld 1.0 Complete Guide</a></li>
        <li><a href="/en/pals/">📖 Pal Database (boss stats and drops)</a></li>
        <li><a href="/en/breeding/">🧮 Breeding Calculator (build the team)</a></li>
        <li><a href="/en/tech-tree/">🔬 Tech Tree (all tech, Lv 1-80)</a></li>
        <li><a href="/en/guides/tech-tree-guide/">📈 Tech Tree Upgrade Route</a></li>
        <li><a href="/en/guides/beginner-guide/">🎮 Beginner Guide</a></li>
      </ul>
    </section>
  </article>
</Layout>

<style>
  .guide-content { max-width: 820px; margin: 0 auto; line-height: 1.8; }
  .page-header { margin-bottom: 2rem; }
  .breadcrumb { color: var(--text-secondary); font-size: 0.9rem; margin-bottom: 0.5rem; }
  .breadcrumb a { color: var(--accent); text-decoration: none; }
  h1 { font-size: 2rem; margin: 0.5rem 0; line-height: 1.3; }
  h2 { font-size: 1.5rem; margin: 2.5rem 0 1rem; color: var(--accent); border-left: 4px solid var(--accent); padding-left: 0.8rem; }
  h3 { font-size: 1.2rem; margin: 1.5rem 0 0.8rem; color: var(--text-primary); }
  p { margin: 0.8rem 0; }
  ul, ol { margin: 0.8rem 0; padding-left: 1.5rem; }
  li { margin: 0.5rem 0; }
  a { color: var(--accent); text-decoration: underline; }
  .related-links { margin-top: 3rem; padding: 1.5rem; background: var(--bg-secondary); border-radius: var(--radius-lg); }
  .related-links ul { list-style: none; padding: 0; }
  .related-links li { padding: 0.3rem 0; }
</style>
